Documentation programs
======================

The .cpp files in this directory exist to be QUOTED BY THE GENEVA MANUAL, and to
PRODUCE THE FIGURES it prints: where the real library code is too heavyweight to
excerpt honestly, the manual shows a small, complete program instead (suite
Invariant 27; rule D2 in the manual repository's DOCUMENTATION-INVARIANTS.md).
Every program here

  * compiles and links against this library's own dependency closure only,
  * runs as an S-size ctest case, so it cannot rot without turning a gate red,
  * carries //! [anchor] snippet markers that the manual's extraction layer
    reads (see source/snippets.map in the manual repository),
  * writes its output to the WORKING DIRECTORY -- never into the source tree --
    so it can be run from a build tree by the manual's `make figures` target.

They are deliberately separate from examples/: an example teaches a user how to
use the library, a documentation program backs a specific listing or figure in
the manual. Treat marker pairs like code -- moving or deleting one breaks the
manual build, which names the affected chapter.

Figure producers
----------------

noisy_parabola_scan   Scans the two-dimensional Berlich noisy parabola on a
                      regular grid and searches it with a small evolutionary
                      algorithm, writing two ROOT macros:
                        noisy-parabola-scan.C      the landscape as a surface,
                                                   with every individual the
                                                   algorithm evaluated drawn on
                                                   top of it in red
                        noisy-parabola-progress.C  the same run seen from the
                                                   optimizer's side (best
                                                   fitness per iteration, and
                                                   the distribution of every
                                                   evaluated fitness)
                      The scan is deterministic; the search is not (Geneva's
                      random numbers are not reproducible from a seed --
                      DEVELOPMENT-INVARIANTS.md, Invariant 18), so a rerun draws
                      a different but equivalent path.
